course: everyday
lesson: 426
title: «The dog ate it»: excuses for homework
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Max in Dublin had played FIFA all evening, so in the morning he told Mrs Kelly: «The dog ate it, miss, swear down.» The whole class roared — everyone knew Max's family has a goldfish and no dog. «Grand,» said Mrs Kelly, «bring me the dog's note then.» He did the homework at lunch and a note still went home, now about the homework AND the lie.
explain: Most homework excuses die the same way: they can be checked. Priya in Leicester said the printer broke, and her teacher said «Lovely, email it tonight then» — there was nothing to email. Leo in Boston blamed the WiFi, but the quiz website showed he had logged in at eight and played games for an hour. «Your WiFi worked fine for the games, buddy.»
explain: Some excuses drag in other people. Ella in Auckland said Mum let her off for «a family thing»; she had really been at the beach all arvo (afternoon). At pick-up the teacher said «Hope the family thing went well», and Mum said «What family thing?» Noah in Toronto said someone stole his homework from his bag, and his mate Leo said «Nah bro, you were on Roblox with me.» Blaming an invisible thief makes the whole class look guilty.
explain: Jayden in Houston even made fake proof: he poured juice on his sheet himself. His teacher smoothed it out — the juice was only on the empty half — and raised one eyebrow. Ruby in Glasgow said her wee (little) brother scribbled on it so she binned it; really she was stuck after one line. Nobody checked, so she got away with it — and the next week the same sums came up in a test and she got a bad mark, because nobody had helped her.
explain: Other stories fell apart too. Kai in Manchester said «I left it at home, sir» on Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday; on Thursday nobody believed it any more, and his teacher rang home. Zara in Cape Town said load-shedding had stopped her, but her teacher checked the power-cut schedule: that evening the cut was in another part of town, not in her area. Theo in Leeds said he had been «proper poorly» (really ill) with a headache, but a classmate had seen him playing football in the ginnel (a narrow alley between houses) that same evening. And when Leo's grandad read the email from Leo's school about the WiFi, he just said «Bummer» — American slang for «that's bad luck».
explain: The plain truth is nearly always the cheapest: «I didn't do it, I'll bring it tomorrow», or «I got stuck». A missing homework is a small problem; a lie in front of the class turns it into a big one.
fact: «The dog ate my homework» is the oldest school excuse, so teachers hear it as a joke.
fact: An excuse that can be checked in one minute usually is checked: a broken printer can be answered with «email it», a WiFi story with the website's record.
fact: Online homework sites usually keep a record of when you logged in and what you did.
fact: Blaming a parent («Mum said I didn't have to») fails because teachers and parents talk to each other.
fact: Making fake proof, like pouring juice on a sheet, turns a fib into a plan to fool someone, and teachers take it more seriously.
fact: Saying «I left it at home» every day is a lie that shows itself, because the homework never comes.
fact: Using a real problem that did not happen to you, like a power cut in another street, is still a lie.
fact: Pretending to be ill makes it harder for people to believe you on a day you really are ill.
fact: Saying «someone stole it» when nobody did makes innocent classmates look suspicious.
fact: A lie that hides «I'm stuck» stops anyone from helping, so the problem comes back in the next test.
fact: The punishment for an excuse is often bigger than for the missing homework: the lie gets its own consequence.
fact: «Swear down» (Irish and British slang) means «I swear it's true»; «load-shedding» in South Africa means planned power cuts.
exercise: Why did the class laugh at Max's excuse? | Everyone knew his family had a goldfish and no dog | Dogs never eat paper of any kind | Mrs Kelly had a dog of her own | Max said it in a funny voice
exercise: What was the real reason Max had no homework? | He had played FIFA all evening | His dog really had chewed it to bits | He had been ill in bed with a cold | He had done it at a friend's house
exercise: How did Priya's teacher check the broken-printer story? | She asked Priya to email it that night | She rang the printer company to ask | She checked Priya's school bag for ink stains | She asked to see the broken printer
exercise: Jayden poured juice on his own sheet. What gave him away? | The juice was only on the blank half | His brother told the teacher at the gate | The juice was the wrong colour for orange | He forgot to bring the sheet
exercise: Why was Ella's «family thing» excuse found out? | The teacher mentioned it to Mum at pick-up | Ella forgot to say it on the day | A friend posted a photo of Ella at the beach online | Ella's brother told the whole class
exercise: Kai said «I left it at home» three days running. What happened on Thursday? | His teacher rang home | His teacher believed him again | His homework turned up at school | His mum brought it in herself
exercise: Noah said someone took his homework from his bag. Who gave him away? | His mate, who had played Roblox with him | The teacher, who searched every bag in the class | A camera in the classroom | His little sister at home
exercise: What did Zara's teacher check to see through the power-cut story? | The power-cut schedule for her area | Zara's torch and how flat its batteries were | A photo of her dark kitchen at night | Her mum's text messages from that evening
exercise: How was Theo's «poorly with a headache» story found out? | A classmate had seen him playing football in the street | His mum rang to say he was fine | The school nurse tested his head | His planner showed a doctor's note
exercise: Ruby got away with her excuse. What did it cost her later? | She failed the same kind of sums in a test | She lost her place in the class quiz team | Her brother got blamed by the teacher | Nothing at all, ever, because nobody found out
exercise: How did Leo's teacher know the WiFi was not down? | The quiz website showed him online playing games | Leo's grandad told the teacher at the school gate | The WiFi company sent a letter | Leo's mate said he had WiFi
exercise: Why is blaming a pretend thief worse than just forgetting? | It makes innocent classmates look guilty | Thieves never take homework | It gets the teacher into trouble with the head | It is exactly the same as forgetting
exercise: Why does a fake illness excuse cause trouble later? | People find it harder to believe you when you are really ill | Fake illness can make you really ill | Teachers stop setting you homework | It makes the illness come true
exercise: What is the cheapest thing to say when you didn't do the homework? | «I didn't do it, I'll bring it tomorrow» | «My dog ate it last night, miss, honestly» | «The printer broke last night just as I pressed print» | «Someone took it out of my bag at break»
exercise: Max said «swear down». What does that mean? | I swear it's true | I'm lying to you, but only a bit | I'm feeling down today | Please calm down and sit down
exercise: Why did Max's punishment end up bigger than for just forgetting? | The lie got its own consequence on top | Teachers punish the dogs too, in a way | He had forgotten twice before that week | The class laughed too loudly
test: Mate: «just say ur printer broke, works every time». Why might it NOT work? | The teacher can just say «email it then» | Printers never break at home | Teachers don't know what printers are | It only works on Mondays, when teachers are tired
test: «miss the wifi was down» — but the quiz site says you logged in at 8. What does the teacher know? | That you were online and the excuse is false | That the website is broken and shows wrong times | That the WiFi went down at 9 | Nothing, websites keep no records
test: Lad in Leeds: «was proper poorly, miss» — but his mate says «you were bossing it in the ginnel last night». What's a ginnel? | A narrow alley between houses | A kind of football boot with studs | A doctor's busy waiting room | A school playground with goals
test: Ella's teacher to Mum: «Hope the family thing went well!» Why is this the end of Ella's story? | Mum knows there was no family thing | The teacher is being rude to Mum | Mum will say it went well, to help Ella out | The family thing was cancelled
test: Ruby binned her homework and nobody asked. Which is TRUE? | She got away with it but stayed stuck | Getting away with it fixed the sums for her | Her brother learned the sums for her | The test was cancelled because of it
test: Zara: «but sir there really ARE power cuts here». What's the point the teacher is making? | A real problem that didn't happen to you is still a lie | Power cuts never happen in Cape Town | Homework must be done by candle | Only teachers may talk about power cuts
test: Jayden: «Look, my brother spilled juice, I've got the proof!» Why is fake proof worse than a fib? | It is a planned way to fool someone | Juice ruins the teacher's desk and books | Proof is always believed | It is exactly the same as a fib
test: «left it at home, sir» on Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday... What would happen if Kai said it on Thursday too? | Nobody would believe it, and the teacher would ring home | The teacher would let him off forever | The homework would appear by itself | Kai would get extra marks
test: Noah: «someone nicked it outta my bag». What is the real harm of this lie? | Innocent kids in the class get suspected | It makes his bag look untidy and messy | It's a compliment to his homework | There is no harm at all, it's just a story
test: Leo's grandad, seeing the email home: «Bummer.» What does he mean? | That's bad luck, too bad | Well done, great work, I'm proud | I'm very angry with you indeed | Let's go to the shop together
test: In a cartoon a boy says the dog ate it, and the teacher believes him. What is closer to real life? | Teachers have heard it so often it's a joke | Teachers always believe stories about dogs | Dogs eat homework most days | The teacher would give him a dog
exam: Identify the principal reason for which an excuse capable of immediate verification is commonly unsuccessful. | The teacher may verify it at once, as by requesting that the work be sent electronically | Teachers are prohibited from believing pupils | Excuses are never permitted in schools | Electronic devices do not malfunction
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the attribution of missing homework to an unidentified thief. | It brings suspicion upon innocent pupils and is regarded as a serious lie | It is a permissible excuse where no thief is named and no pupil is accused | It ensures that the work is excused | It places responsibility upon the teacher
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an excuse that remains undiscovered but conceals that the pupil did not understand the work? | The pupil receives no assistance and the difficulty recurs in subsequent assessment | The difficulty is thereby resolved | The pupil acquires the knowledge in question | The excuse is subsequently rewarded
exam: Determine the most appropriate statement by a pupil who has failed to complete homework. | An admission that the work was not done, with an undertaking to submit it | An assertion that a domestic animal consumed it | An assertion that a parent authorised its omission | An assertion that it was removed by another pupil
